ment":["We\u0027re overriding the sort field for OTel data streams. Should we adjust the sort order for OTel, too?\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/d7fa7405d427340b99ff97fcd37ecb3d1422c22d/x-pack/plugin/otel-data/src/main/resources/component-templates/logs-otel%40mappings.yaml#L7-L11","I think so, esp if we want to process the latest entries more efficiently.."],"path":"server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/index/IndexSortConfig.java","id":37294,"created_at":"2024-12-30T12:57:10Z","original_position":25,"side":"RIGHT","position":25,"submitted_on_commit":"680a023e3fefe0d79affdcc81a0f7e146b1c847e","diff_hunk":"@@ -103,12 +102,23 @@ public final class IndexSortConfig {\n         Setting.Property.ServerlessPublic\n     );\n \n-    public static final FieldSortSpec[] TIME_SERIES_SORT;\n-\n+    public static final FieldSortSpec[] TIME_SERIES_SORT, TIMESTAMP_SORT, HOSTNAME_TIMESTAMP_SORT, HOSTNAME_TIMESTAMP_BWC_SORT;\n     static {\n         FieldSortSpec timeStampSpec \u003d new FieldSortSpec(DataStreamTimestampFieldMapper.DEFAULT_PATH);\n         timeStampSpec.order \u003d SortOrder.DESC;\n         TIME_SERIES_SORT \u003d new FieldSortSpec[] { new FieldSortSpec(TimeSeriesIdFieldMapper.NAME), timeStampSpec };\n+        TIMESTAMP_SORT \u003d new FieldSortSpec[] { timeStampSpec };\n+\n+        FieldSortSpec hostnameSpec \u003d new FieldSortSpec(IndexMode.HOST_NAME);\n+        hostnameSpec.order \u003d SortOrder.DESC;\n+        hostnameSpec.missingValue \u003d \"_last\";\n+        hostnameSpec.mode \u003d MultiValueMode.MIN;\n+        HOSTNAME_TIMESTAMP_SORT \u003d new FieldSortSpec[] { hostnameSpec, timeStampSpec };","submitted_line":116},"generatedCommentsNodes":[]},{"groundTruth":{"original_line":714,"line":722,"comment_lines":["[][722]"],"review_comment":["Just to verify my understanding of why we need this setting. Is it for cases where the mapping does contain a `host.name` field but the user doesn\u0027t want it to be used for sorting purposes? I suppose that is to have parity with the previous behavior where you could disable sorting by `host.name` by manually specifying just `@timestamp` as a sort field. Did I get that right?\r\n\r\nNit: there\u0027s a discrepancy in the naming (`SORT_ON_HOST_NAME` vs `use_default_sort_config`). The latter seems a bit more generic in case we add more fields to the default sort config. But either way, I think it makes sense to keep the name of the config option and the constant aligned.","The idea here is that, if we detect that the mappings contain a field (not object) `host.name`, it\u0027s used for sorting. Otherwise, we only sort on `@timestamp`. The rationale is that there\u0027s no point to inject `host.name` and sort on it if the mappings don\u0027t indicate that it\u0027s already present and used.\r\n\r\nGood catch about the discrepancy - initially I had 2 settings to separately control injecting `host.name` and sorting on it. It feels cleaner to skip injection, but I\u0027m really interested on your take here.","As mentioned in https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/pull/118968#discussion_r1899523137, I think it\u0027s important to still support dynamically mapped `host.name` fields. This could either be achieved by injecting `host.name` or by introducing this sorting hash we talked about.","Wouldn\u0027t `host.name` get injected from a dynamic template in this case? What if `host` gets dynamically mapped to a keyword as well, without `subobjects:false`?","\u003e Wouldn\u0027t host.name get injected from a dynamic template in this case?\r\n\r\nNot entirely sure what you mean. But there doesn\u0027t need to be a dynamic template that\u0027s specific to `host.name`, it could just be one that maps strings to keywords by default.\r\n\r\n\u003e What if host gets dynamically mapped to a keyword as well, without subobjects:false?\r\n\r\nThat would be an issue indeed. Still better than the current default that always injects the host.name. I suppose we\u0027ll eventually need a solution that doesn\u0027t depend on injecting a mapping but also fully supports dynamic mappings. The only solution that comes to my mind that reconciles the two is the sorting hash approach.","ok I\u0027ve restored the second setting for injecting `host.name` when possible."],"path":"server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/index/IndexSettings.java","id":37293,"created_at":"2024-12-30T12:34:53Z","original_position":4,"side":"RIGHT","position":12,"submitted_on_commit":"680a023e3fefe0d79affdcc81a0f7e146b1c847e","diff_hunk":"@@ -711,6 +711,13 @@ public boolean isES87TSDBCodecEnabled() {\n         Property.Final\n     );\n \n+    public static final Setting\u003cBoolean\u003e LOGSDB_SORT_ON_HOST_NAME \u003d Setting.boolSetting(","submitted_line":714},"generatedCommentsNodes":[{"comment":"The introduction of `LOGSDB_SORT_ON_HOST_NAME` setting and its usage in various places seems consistent. However, the logic for determining whether to sort on `host.name` should be clearly documented or have comments explaining why certain conditions are checked (e.g., custom sort config presence). The refactoring of `IndexSortConfig` constructor introduces additional checks that could be simplified. Specifically, the repeated checks for the existence of settings (`INDEX_SORT_ORDER_SETTING.exists(settings)`, etc.) before throwing exceptions can be streamlined by combining them into a single validation step. The method `getAdditionalIndexSettings` now includes logic to inject sorting on `host.name`. This change is clear but could benefit from additional test cases that cover scenarios where the setting is explicitly set to false or when there are conflicting settings. The new tests added (`testSortOnHostNamePropagateValue`, `testSortOnHostNameWithCustomSortConfig`, etc.) are comprehensive and cover various scenarios. However, it would be beneficial to add a test case where the `host.name` field is not present in the mappings at all to ensure that the logic correctly handles this 